# Dev guidelines

How to navigate and extend this codebase. A fresh contributor should read this
file before adding code, so that new contributions land where the rest of the
project would expect them. This file is jointly maintained by `/refactor`
(style + idioms + anti-patterns) and `/unspaghettify` (file roles + dependency
direction + placement); each command MERGES into its own sections without
clobbering the other's.

## Module roles (one sentence each)

Grouped by layer, top of the dependency graph last.

### Leaves — depend on nothing in-project

- `launch/utils.py` — domain-neutral helpers (formatting, sorting, parsing, host:port split).
- `launch/paths.py` — every host + container path the launcher knows; the *only* place paths get defined.

### Disk + identity — depend only on leaves

- `launch/file_access.py` — sole chokepoint for every disk-touching call (reads, writes, removes, queries, lists, stats).
- `launch/structs.py` — identity dataclasses (`AgentIdentity` → `InstanceIdentity` → `InstanceIdentity`) + `InstanceModifiers` enum taxonomy.

### Small services — depend on disk + identity

- `launch/claude_code_config.py` — Claude-Code-side display setup the launcher stages from the host (status line + terminal title).
- `launch/memory_addendums.py` — launch-time addendums composed into per-instance `CLAUDE.md` (per modifier in the active chain).
- `launch/network.py` — `{auto}`-mode firewall: host-side DNS cascade + cross-launch IP cache + streaming iptables updater.

### Orchestration — depend on services

- `launch/compose_env.py` — compose env-var staging (`ComposeEnvKey` taxonomy, `_compose_env` accumulator, `-e` flag emission).
- `launch/docker_config.py` — docker subprocess wrappers + bind-mount accumulator + image-chain naming + `run_compose`.
- `launch/agent_modifiers_handler.py` — chain composition via `_apply_<modifier>` dispatch + `{auto}+{DooD}` warning + cache lifecycle.
- `launch/agents_crud.py` — instance state CRUD (create/modify/delete) + workspace + modes-map writers + picker-entry builders + sort keys.
- `launch/user_additions.py` — user-side contributions to the container (optional-creds mounts + first-launch template plants).
- `launch/audit.py` — read-only state-correctness checker, runnable as `python -m launch.audit`.

### UI

- `launch/menu_picker.py` — prompt_toolkit picker + line prompts (workspace, session, modes) + pre-launch banner.

### Entry

- `run.py` — `launch()` 7-stage orchestrator (input → fs-validate → resume → persist → categorise → setup → run).

### Other directories

- `launch/templates/` — source-side files `file_access.copy_file` plants to `user_extras/` on first launch.
- `launch/tests/` — unittest suite (`python3 -m unittest discover -s launch/tests` from project root).
- `agents/` — agent definitions (`<name>[tag](parent).md` + optional matching `.conf`).
- `docker/` — `Dockerfile.<step>` + `compose.<step>.yml` per modifier; `init-firewall.sh` + `auto-entrypoint.sh` for `{auto}`.
- `settings/` — in-container bashrc + status line + manifest helpers + Claude settings/keybindings (bind-mounted via `DOCKER_BASE_MOUNTS`).
- `custom_commands/` — launcher-bundled slash commands (bind-mounted into every container).
- `custom_skills/` — launcher-bundled skills (bind-mounted as a single dir; each `<name>/SKILL.md` becomes a `/<name>` skill).

## Dependency direction

The arrows must stay one-way. A reversal creates a cycle (or hides one behind
lazy imports, which is the bandage of last resort).

```
utils ──► paths ──► file_access ──► structs ──┐
                                              ├─► small services (claude_code_config, memory_addendums, network)
                                              │
                            small services ──► orchestration (compose_env, docker_config, agent_modifiers_handler,
                                                              agents_crud, user_additions, audit)
                                                       │
                                                       ▼
                                                       UI (menu_picker)
                                                       │
                                                       ▼
                                                       entry (run.py)
```

**One acknowledged cycle:** `docker_config ↔ network`. `docker_config` imports
`network` at module level (for `is_critical_pending` / `start_firewall_updater` /
`wait_for_critical_addresses`); `network._updater_worker` and
`network._insert_iptables_accept` lazy-import `docker_config.wait_for_container_running`
and `docker_config.docker_exec_root` from inside the function body. Both
docstrings call this out. Don't add new lazy imports without good reason —
prefer to push the shared logic to a leaf-er module first.

## Where new code goes — by example

For each common addition, place it where the rest of the project would expect.
"X belongs in Y because Z" — the *because* matters more than the rule.

### A new host or container path

→ `paths.py`. Because every path constant in the project lives here — search
finds it once, edit-via-relocation is one line. Static composite paths go
inline with their constituents; dynamic paths get a `<name>_path` lambda at
the bottom.

### A new bind-mount

→ one of the `DOCKER_*_MOUNTS` dicts in `paths.py`. Because the per-launch
mount-staging code (`docker_config.set_container_mounts` and the `_apply_<modifier>`
handlers in `agent_modifiers_handler`) just iterates these dicts — new entry is a
data-side change, not a code change. Pick the dict by scope: `DOCKER_BASE_MOUNTS`
(every launch), `DOCKER_AUTO_MOUNTS` (`{auto}` only), `DOCKER_DOOD_MOUNTS` (`{DooD}`
only), `CACHE_MOUNTS` (`[code]` cache shares).

### A new modifier (tag or mode)

Five touch points, in this order:
1. Append an `InstanceModifiers` enum member in `structs.py` (declaration order
   = chain order). Prefix `TAG_` for filename-grammar tags, `MODE_` for
   per-instance modes.
2. Add `docker/Dockerfile.<slug>` + `docker/compose.<slug>.yml`.
3. Add `_apply_<slug>()` handler + one conditional in `compose_chain` in
   `agent_modifiers_handler.py`.
4. (Optional) `Addendum(...)` instance in `memory_addendums.py` + entry under
   the modifier in `MODIFIER_ADDENDUMS` if you want a launch-time CLAUDE.md
   directive.
5. (Optional) prompt + the mode in `menu_picker.prompt_modes` if it's a per-
   instance mode (user opt-in).

`launch/tests/test_essential_files.py` enforces 1–3 by asserting every non-BASE
member has matching Dockerfile + compose + `_apply_<slug>` handler.

### A new disk-touching helper

→ `file_access.py`. Because that's the project's chokepoint — no other module
calls `open()`, `Path.exists()`, `shutil.*`, `subprocess` for git/sudo, etc.
The chokepoint rule is what makes the rest of the codebase mock-able and
keeps OS-quirk handling (root-owned bind-mount leftovers, etc.) in one place.

### A new env var staged to compose / container

→ `compose_env.py`. If it's a known static key, add it to the `ComposeEnvKey`
enum and stage via `stage_compose_env(key, value)`. If it's forwarded from
the host shell, add to `CONTAINER_ENV_FORWARDS`. If it's a constant fixed at
launch, add to `CONTAINER_ENV_FIXED`. Dynamic INSTALL_<TOOL> / token vars
derive from `OPTIONAL_CREDS_MOUNTS` and `OPTIONAL_CREDS_TOKEN_ENV_VARS` in
`paths.py` — adding a new optional-cred service flows through both.

### A new launch-time CLAUDE.md directive

→ `memory_addendums.py`. Add an `Addendum(title=..., body=...)` instance,
then list it under the relevant modifier in `MODIFIER_ADDENDUMS`. `composed_addendum(chain)`
picks it up via `chain` membership and renders it as a `### <title>` section
under the single `## Launch-time addendums` block. Bodies can `format(...)`
in path constants from `paths.py`.

### A new picker row kind

→ `menu_picker.py`. Define a dataclass for the row's data (see `ContEntry` /
`PickerEntry`). Add a `PickerRowMarker` enum member if you want a new glyph
prefix. Build entries via the existing `pick_with_preview` primitive — it's
generic over `PickerEntry.value`.

### A new state-correctness check

→ `audit.py`. Add a check function (the existing `_modes_map_issues`,
`_check_json_file` are unit-testable in isolation) and call it from `main()`.
Tests go in `launch/tests/test_audit.py`.

### A new identity property derived from existing fields

→ a `@property` on the appropriate identity class in `structs.py`. Don't
add a free function elsewhere — the dataclass owns its derivations. Same
for any "compute X from inst_id" — it's `inst_id.x`, not `compute_x(inst_id)`.

### A new helper that's pure (no disk, no third-party)

→ `utils.py`. Domain-neutral helpers only. If it needs disk, it goes in
`file_access.py`. If it needs an identity dataclass, it's a property /
method on that class.

## Anti-patterns

Things to NOT do, with the reason:

- **Don't import `Path` or call `open()` outside `file_access.py`.** The
  chokepoint rule is what keeps disk-quirk handling (sudo fallbacks, atomic
  writes, JSON-map caching) in one place. New disk reader → new function in
  `file_access.py`.
- **Don't add a constant `FOO_PATH` outside `paths.py`.** Search returns one
  hit for any path; that's the point.
- **Don't add a free function that takes a `InstanceIdentity` and computes
  one of its fields.** That's a `@property` on the dataclass — same call site
  shape, lives next to the rest of the identity surface.
- **Don't introduce a new cycle (even a lazy one).** The existing `network
  ↔ docker_config` cycle is a documented exception; new ones get pushed to
  a leaf-er module first.
- **Don't add a `# noqa` without naming the rule.** `# noqa: E402` not bare
  `# noqa`. Same for `# type: ignore[misc]` over bare `# type: ignore`.
- **Don't re-export by importing-and-not-using.** If module A needs `X` and
  `X` lives in module C, import directly: `from .C import X`. Routing it
  through B (`from .B import X`) is a re-export hop with no value.
